; Test - assertions, and the effect an assertion failure raises.
;
; WHAT AN ASSERTION IS HERE. A failing assertion is programmer error,
; which `docs/error-model.md` puts in exactly one of its three classes:
; not expected failure (that is `Result`, `ERR-TYPE-1`), and not a
; memory-safety fault (no language contains one). Programmer error is
; the only class an in-process abort can serve, and since 2026-08-24
; there is a mechanism for it - `ERR-REC-6`'s recovery point. So a
; failing assertion does not set a flag, does not return a status the
; caller has to thread, and does not exit: it PERFORMS an operation of
; the `Assert` effect, and with no handler in dynamic extent that is
; the unhandled-effect trap, which answers the nearest recovery point
; with 71 and stops the program when there is none.
;
; That single decision is what makes `axiom test` a runner rather than
; a convention:
;
;   - Inside a test, the first failed assertion ENDS the test. Nothing
;     after it runs, so a test cannot report a second, derived failure
;     that only exists because the first one left the world wrong.
;   - The runner arms one recovery point per test (`main` as `axiom
;     test` generates it), so one failing test does not end the run.
;   - A test that traps for any OTHER reason - out of memory, a
;     division by zero - lands in the same place with a different
;     status, so the runner reports it as a failure rather than dying.
;     Measured while this module was written: a test whose body is
;     `(/ 1 (- 1 1))` is reported `FAIL ... (status 72)` and the three
;     tests around it still run.
;   - Outside a runner an assertion is still live. `axiom run` on a
;     file with a bare failing assertion prints the detail, then
;     `axiom: unhandled effect` on fd 2, and exits 71.
;
; The detail line is printed BEFORE the operation is performed, and on
; stdout rather than stderr. Before, because the abort does not return
; and nothing runs on the way out (`ERR-REC-6`) - a detail printed
; "after" would never be printed at all. On stdout, because the
; runner's own `ok`/`FAIL` lines are there and a reader wants one
; ordered stream rather than two that a pipe may interleave.
;
; WHY EVERY ASSERTION TAKES A LABEL. `(assertEq want got)` with the
; expression text recovered from the source is what a macro would give
; in a language whose macros can stringify an arbitrary form. Axiom's
; cannot - `syntax/name` names an identifier, and there is no
; `syntax/text` (`macro-system.md` MAC-CAP-7) - so the choice is an
; explicit label or a failure that says only "want 3, got 4" with
; nothing to say WHERE. The label is the honest half.
;
; WHY THE COMPARISONS ARE MONOMORPHIC. `(:: assertEq (-> String a a
; Int))` would type-check and could not print its operands: `println`
; is a macro over `show`, whose implementation is keyed on a type NAME
; (`AX3025`), so a type variable has no instance to select. One
; function per printable type is the same trade `Show` already makes.
(import IO)

(import Str)

; The effect a failed assertion performs.
;
; Declared without `pub` because an `effect` is exported
; unconditionally - it is the one exception to the `pub` rule
; (reference.md, Modules). An importer calls `assertFail` without
; naming it in an import list, which is what makes the assertions
; below usable from a test file that imports only `Test`.
;
; The operation answers `Int` and no handler here ever returns one.
; A program MAY install a handler - `(handle BODY (Assert) (lambda (s)
; 0))` turns every failed assertion into a value and lets the test
; carry on - and that is a deliberate escape for a test whose whole
; subject is a failing assertion. `tests/tools/` uses it.
(effect Assert
  (assertFail :: (-> String Int)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The assertions
;
; `want` and `got` are in that order in every one of these, and the
; message says which is which, because the commonest way to misread a
; failure is to read the two numbers the wrong way round.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Two `Int`s are equal.
(pub :: assertEq (-> String Int Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (assertEq label want got)
  (if (== want got)
    0
    {
      (println "     {label}: want {want}, got {got}")
      (assertFail label)
    }
  )
)

; Two `Int`s are not equal - for the property that a value CHANGED,
; where naming what it changed to would pin something the test does
; not mean to pin.
(pub :: assertNe (-> String Int Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (assertNe label unwanted got)
  (if (!= unwanted got)
    0
    {
      (println "     {label}: want anything but {unwanted}, got it")
      (assertFail label)
    }
  )
)

; Two `String`s are equal, by bytes.
(pub :: assertStrEq (-> String String String Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (assertStrEq label want got)
  (if (strEq want got)
    0
    {
      (println "     {label}: want \"{want}\", got \"{got}\"")
      (assertFail label)
    }
  )
)

; A `Bool` is true.
(pub :: assertTrue (-> String Bool Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (assertTrue label b)
  (if b
    0
    {
      (println "     {label}: want true, got false")
      (assertFail label)
    }
  )
)

; A `Bool` is false. Not `(assertTrue label (! b))`, because Axiom has
; no `!` and `(== b false)` at the call site is what this exists to
; keep out of the test.
(pub :: assertFalse (-> String Bool Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (assertFalse label b)
  (if b
    {
      (println "     {label}: want false, got true")
      (assertFail label)
    }
    0
  )
)

; Fail unconditionally: the branch that must not be reached, and the
; case a test has not written yet. `(testFail "todo: the empty input")`
; reads as a failure rather than as a passing test with nothing in it,
; which is what an empty test body is.
;
; Spelled `testFail` and not `fail` because `fail` is a name a test
; file is likely to want for its own subject.
(pub :: testFail (-> String Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (testFail label)
  {
    (println "     {label}")
    (assertFail label)
  }
)
